### 8.2 Virtue/Sin discrepancy model

The Virtue/Sin framework should be implemented as an explainable analytical model.

Virtue indicators:

* Verified transaction completion.

* Accurate documentation.

* Consistent fulfillment.

* Valid cryptographic signatures.

* Transparent corrections.

* Reliable source attribution.

Sin indicators:

* Confirmed fraudulent activity.

* Repeated documented defaults.

* Proven message tampering.

* Fabricated transaction evidence.

* Unauthorized access.

* Deliberate misrepresentation established through review.

The model must distinguish an observation from an allegation and an allegation from an established finding.

### 8.3 Proposed scoring dimensions

| Dimension                 | Measurement                   |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Evidence Integrity        | Completeness and verification |
| Transaction Reliability   | Fulfillment performance       |
| Communication Reliability | Delivery and acknowledgment   |
| Source Quality            | Provenance and freshness      |
| Dispute Resolution        | Resolution outcomes           |
| Operational Resilience    | Availability and recovery     |

No single composite score should conceal the underlying measurements.

Participants must be able to challenge inaccurate records and request correction.

### 9\. Tri-Layer Integrity Ledger

The ledger preserves documentary and operational evidence.

### Proposed layers

Layer 1 — Operational Records

* Messages.

* Call Card events.

* Offers and requests.

* Location permissions.

* Transaction state changes.

Layer 2 — Cryptographic Evidence

* Hashes.

* Digital signatures.

* Event sequence references.

* Merkle proofs.

* Verification results.

Layer 3 — Documentary Audit

* Event provenance.

* Administrative decisions.

* Dispute outcomes.

* Corrections.

* Exportable audit reports.

The ledger should preserve integrity without requiring every private message or sensitive location record to be publicly visible.

Cryptographic hashing establishes consistency with recorded data; it does not independently establish that the original information was truthful.

